### 3. Hierarchy of Latency (Context)

To sound like a Staff Engineer, you must know where "3 ns" sits in the hierarchy of speed. It is slow compared to math, but fast compared to memory.

| Operation | Cycles (Approx) | Time (Approx) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **CPU Register Access** | 0 cycles | Immediate |
| **Simple Math (Add/Sub)** | 1 cycle | ~0.3 ns |
| **Branch Mispredict** | **~10-20 cycles** | **~3 ns** |
| **L1 Cache Reference** | ~3-4 cycles | ~1 ns |
| **L2 Cache Reference** | ~10-12 cycles | ~4 ns |
| **Mutex Lock/Unlock** | ~25 cycles | ~10 ns |
| **Main Memory (RAM)** | ~200-300 cycles | **~100 ns** |

**Key Takeaway for Interviews:**
If you are designing a system and you have a choice between a **Branch Mispredict (3ns)** and a **RAM Access (100ns)**, you always take the mispredict. Computing is cheap; Memory is expensive.